  "question": "Why should I pay more attention to Artwork A1 than Artwork A2, and what evidence supports that?",
  "answer_type": "answer",
  "answer": "On the processed days, A1 has stronger evidence of a lasting rise in views than A2. Both artworks are still flagged as sustained growth, so A2 is not being ignored. A1's evidence is just better supported.\n\n**Views:** A1 went from 40 to 80 between 2026-10-14 and 2026-10-15, a change of +40 (+100.0%). It stayed high at 76 on 2026-10-16 and 90 on 2026-10-17. A2 went from 25 to 35, a change of +10 (+40.0%). It then had 32 and 38 on the later days. A2's rise just clears the minimum thresholds. The minimums are +30% and +10 views, and A2 is at +40% and +10.\n\n**Corroborating metrics (2026-10-14 to 2026-10-15):**\n- Daily unique viewers for A1 rose from 30 to 45, a change of +15 (+50.0%). For A2 they rose from 20 to 26, a change of +6 (+30.0%). Both counted as support.\n- Favourites for A1 rose from 5 to 12, a change of +7 (+140.0%). That counted as support. For A2 they rose from 3 to 4, a change of +1 (+33.3%). That is below the +3 minimum, so it did not count.\n- Inquiries were 0 on both days for both artworks, so there is no change. A percentage can't be calculated from a zero baseline.\n\nA1 is corroborated by two other metrics and A2 by one. That is why A1 is labelled higher-confidence and A2 moderate-confidence.",
  "limitations": [
    "These are screening rules on four processed days (2026-10-14 to 2026-10-17). They are not statistical tests, and the confidence labels only reflect how many metrics support the pattern.",
    "The data shows what happened, not why. It does not show purchase intent, sales or who the viewers were.",
    "Views and favourites are event counts, not people. Daily unique viewers count people for one artwork on one day only and should not be added across days.",
    "2026-10-18 is not processed and is excluded from all of this."
  ],
